{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714490,"nonce":"AtQuGDI7S0He+aGDlnnsAHy4HlE=","title":"jno.solve.imex(cfl=): reverse-mode differentiation with respect to the start state fails on a traced substep count","message":"**What goes wrong.** An IMEX march with `cfl=` takes its number of substeps per step from the CFL limit of the state. When the start state is traced, that count is traced too, and it becomes the bound of a `lax.fori_loop`. Reverse mode cannot differentiate a `fori_loop` with a dynamic bound, so `jax.grad` and `jax.jacrev` of the march with respect to the start state raise. Forward mode and plain `jit` work.\n\n**Reproduce.** A heat problem `u_t = 0.05 (u_xx + u_yy) + s sin(pi x)` on `jno.shape.rect(0, 0, 1, 1, size=0.25).domain(time=(0, 0.2, 5))` as a `jno.fvm` problem `p`, `x0` its initial state (44 values), then:\n\n```python\nsch = jno.solve.imex(\"ars222\", cfl=0.5)\nf = lambda x: jnp.sum(jnp.asarray(p.solve(time=sch, x0=x)) ** 2)\njax.jacfwd(f)(x0)                  # works\njax.jit(f)(x0)                     # works\njax.grad(f)(x0)                    # fails\njax.jit(jax.grad(f))(x0)           # fails\n```\n\nMeasured on main, CPU, float64: the failing calls raise `ValueError: Reverse-mode differentiation does not work for lax.while_loop or lax.fori_loop with dynamic start/stop values`, from the `fori_loop` in the IMEX `integrate` (jno/utils/solver/imex.py). The explicit scheme `jno.solve.erk(\"ssprk3\", cfl=0.5)` differentiates in reverse mode eagerly (`grad`, `jacrev` ran fine); it fails under `jit` with a traced start for an unrelated reason, so I found no reverse-mode failure for ERK.\n\n**Expected.** A reverse-mode gradient with respect to the start state, or a refusal that names `cfl=` and the way out. This is the gradient an inverse problem on the initial condition needs.\n\n**Where.** jno/utils/solver/imex.py `integrate`, the traced branch: `sub` from `cfl_dt(stop_gradient(s0))` feeds `run(u, t, sub)`, a `fori_loop(0, sub, ...)`. The explicit scheme in jno/utils/solver/timeschemes.py has the same structure.\n\n**Workaround.** Drop `cfl=` and set the step with `domain.time` (no substeps), or differentiate in forward mode.\n\n**Direction.** Since the count comes from `stop_gradient` of the start state, it carries no derivative: either compute it concretely once outside the differentiated region, or use a fixed upper bound with a masked `scan`, which reverse mode accepts.","files":null}]}